object CodePointRepository {
    // Stored as chunked string literals rather than one intArrayOf(...) of
    // every character's code point -- see buildKotlinSource in lawgpt.html
    // for why.
    private val textChunks: List<String> = listOf(
        "[0m[1m1. Rules[0m\n[0m[1mRule: Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(A)[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Federal Rule of Civil Procedure, cited in Daimler AG\nv. Bauman, Part II\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Federal courts ordinarily follow state law in\ndetermining the bounds of their jurisdiction over persons;\nservice of process establishes personal jurisdiction over a\ndefendant \"who is subject to the jurisdiction of a court of\ngeneral jurisdiction in the state where the district court is\nlocated\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Which law sets the outer bounds of a federal\ndistrict court's personal jurisdiction over a defendant?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The defendant must be subject to the\njurisdiction of a court of general jurisdiction in the state\nwhere the district court sits\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the first step in any federal\npersonal-jurisdiction analysis; the federal court borrows the\nforum state's reach, so in Daimler the Northern District of\nCalifornia asked how far California's own courts could reach\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The rule applies \"ordinarily,\" which signals that\nother bases may exist; the reading does not cover them\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Rule 4(k)(1)(A) points to state law, but the\nstate long-arm statute and the federal Due Process Clause still\nhave to be checked\n\n[0m[1mRule: Cal. Civ. Proc. Code § 410.10[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: California long-arm statute, cited in Daimler AG v.\nBauman, Part II\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: California courts may exercise personal\njurisdiction \"on any basis not inconsistent with the\nConstitution of this state or of the United States,\" which\nallows personal jurisdiction to the full extent the U.S.\nConstitution permits\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How far does California's long-arm statute\nreach?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The exercise of jurisdiction must not be\ninconsistent with the California Constitution or the U.S.\nConstitution\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Because the statute reaches as far as the\nConstitution allows, the statutory question and the\nconstitutional question become one; in Daimler, the Supreme\nCourt therefore went straight to whether the Ninth Circuit's\nholding \"comports with the limits imposed by federal due\nprocess\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The statute is limited by constitutional due\nprocess; it cannot authorize jurisdiction the Constitution\nforbids\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: When a long-arm statute reaches to the\nconstitutional limit, the analysis collapses into one step; the\nreading does not discuss narrower statutes\n\n[0m[1mRule: General and specific jurisdiction distinction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding of Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\nv. Brown, as restated and applied in Daimler AG v. Bauman,\nIntroduction and Part III\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Personal jurisdiction is either general\n(all-purpose) jurisdiction, which lets a court hear \"any and all\nclaims\" against a defendant, or specific (conduct-linked)\njurisdiction, which depends on a link between the forum and the\nclaim\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Which category of personal jurisdiction is\nbeing asserted, and what test governs it?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Identify whether the claim arises from or relates to the\n    defendant's forum activities, which is the\n    specific-jurisdiction track\n    2. If it does not, jurisdiction must rest on general\n    jurisdiction, which requires that the defendant be \"at home\"\n    in the forum\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the threshold sorting question; in\nDaimler, plaintiffs \"never attempted to fit this case into the\nspecific jurisdiction category,\" so the whole case turned on\ngeneral jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Parties can effectively choose which theory to\nlitigate by what they raise or concede below; the Court took the\nparties' choices \"as given\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: General jurisdiction asks about the defendant's\nrelationship to the forum, regardless of the claim; specific\njurisdiction asks about the relationship among the defendant,\nthe forum, and the particular claim\n\n[0m[1mRule: Divergent trajectories of general and specific[0m\n[0m[1mjurisdiction after International Shoe[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case narrative in Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part III, and\nthe textbook's bullet-point summary following the opinion\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Since International Shoe, \"specific jurisdiction\nhas become the centerpiece of modern jurisdiction theory, while\ngeneral jurisdiction has played a reduced role\"; specific\njurisdiction \"has been cut loose from Pennoyer's sway,\" but the\nCourt has \"declined to stretch general jurisdiction beyond\nlimits traditionally recognized\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Has the post-International Shoe expansion of\npersonal jurisdiction applied equally to general and specific\njurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Not applicable, because this is a framing\nprinciple rather than a test with elements\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The Court uses this principle to resist arguments\nthat general jurisdiction should grow along with specific\njurisdiction; it explains why the Daimler Court read general\njurisdiction narrowly while acknowledging the \"rapid expansion\"\nof jurisdiction \"when the episode-in-suit occurred in the forum\nor the defendant purposefully availed itself of the forum\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated in the reading\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: International Shoe broke from Pennoyer's \"rigidly\nterritorial focus,\" but the Court treats that break as mainly a\nspecific-jurisdiction development\n\n[0m[1mRule: Specific jurisdiction under International Shoe[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: International Shoe, 326 U.S. at 317, as quoted and\ndescribed in Daimler AG v. Bauman, Parts III and IV.B\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Jurisdiction can be asserted where a corporation's\nin-state activities are not only \"continuous and systematic, but\nalso give rise to the liabilities sued on\"; more broadly,\ntribunals may hear claims against out-of-state defendants \"when\nthe episode-in-suit occurred in the forum or the defendant\npurposefully availed itself of the forum\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a court hear a claim against an\nout-of-state defendant because of the connection between the\nclaim and the forum?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: As described in the reading, a connection\nbetween the claim and the forum, shown by either:\n    1. The episode-in-suit occurred in the forum, or\n    2. The defendant purposefully availed itself of the forum,\n    with the in-state activities giving rise to the liabilities\n    sued on\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The Court cites this rule mainly to show that the\nphrase \"continuous and systematic\" in International Shoe\ndescribed specific jurisdiction, not general jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not discuss limits on specific\njurisdiction; it was not at issue because plaintiffs never\ninvoked it\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: \"Continuous and systematic\" contacts that give\nrise to the claim support specific jurisdiction; \"continuous and\nsystematic\" contacts alone, without a link to the claim, do not\nestablish general jurisdiction\n\n[0m[1mRule: At home standard for general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding of Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\nv. Brown, 564 U.S. 915 (2011), as applied in Daimler AG v.\nBauman, Introduction and Part IV.B\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A court may assert general jurisdiction over a\nforeign corporation \"to hear any and all claims against it\" only\nwhen the corporation's affiliations with the forum state are \"so\n'continuous and systematic' as to render it essentially at home\nin the forum State\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When can a corporation be sued in a forum on\nclaims that have nothing to do with the forum?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The corporation's affiliations with the forum state must\n    be so constant and pervasive\n    2. That the corporation is \"essentially at home\" in the\n    forum state\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Invoked whenever a plaintiff sues on a claim\nunconnected to the forum; the court asks whether the forum is\none of the limited places where the corporation is \"fairly\nregarded as at home,\" and not merely whether the corporation\ndoes a lot of business there\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Goodyear \"did not hold that a corporation may be\nsubject to general jurisdiction only in a forum where it is\nincorporated or has its principal place of business\"; other\nat-home forums are possible, but Daimler does not identify any\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The question is not whether contacts are \"in some\nsense 'continuous and systematic'\" but whether they make the\ncorporation \"essentially at home\"; the qualifier \"essentially at\nhome\" matters\n\n[0m[1mRule: Paradigm forums for general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Goodyear, 564 U.S. at 924, as quoted and applied in\nDaimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV.B, with Hertz Corp. v. Friend, 559\nU.S. 77, 94 (2010), cited for the value of simple jurisdictional\nrules\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"For an individual, the paradigm forum for the\nexercise of general jurisdiction is the individual's domicile;\nfor a corporation, it is an equivalent place, one in which the\ncorporation is fairly regarded as at home,\" and the place of\nincorporation and principal place of business are the \"paradigm\nbases for general jurisdiction\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Where is a defendant clearly subject to\nall-purpose jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. For an individual: the forum is the individual's domicile\n    2. For a corporation: the forum is the place of\n    incorporation or the principal place of business\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: These bases are attractive because each is\n\"unique,\" meaning it ordinarily indicates only one place, and\n\"easily ascertainable\"; they guarantee plaintiffs \"at least one\nclear and certain forum in which a corporate defendant may be\nsued on any and all claims\"; in Daimler, neither Daimler nor\nMBUSA was incorporated in California or had its principal place\nof business there\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: These are \"paradigm\" or \"exemplar\" bases, not an\nexclusive list\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: These bases come from the defendant's legal or\norganizational identity, not from how much business it does in\nthe forum\n\n[0m[1mRule: Substantial, continuous, and systematic course of business[0m\n[0m[1minsufficient for general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding of Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV.B\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A corporation is not subject to general\njurisdiction in every state in which it \"engages in a\nsubstantial, continuous, and systematic course of business\"; the\nCourt held that formulation \"unacceptably grasping\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Does a large, ongoing volume of in-state\nbusiness by itself make a corporation subject to all-purpose\njurisdiction there?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Not applicable, because this rule rejects a\nproposed test; the operative test remains whether the\ncorporation is \"essentially at home\"\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Used to defeat plaintiffs' arguments that heavy\nin-state sales or facilities create general jurisdiction; in\nDaimler, even substantial California activity (MBUSA as the\nlargest luxury-vehicle supplier in California, with multiple\nfacilities) did not suffice; if it did, \"the same global reach\nwould presumably be available in every other State in which\nMBUSA's sales are sizable\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated in the reading\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: International Shoe's \"continuous and systematic\"\nlanguage described specific jurisdiction; for general\njurisdiction, International Shoe required operations \"so\nsubstantial and of such a nature as to justify suit … on causes\nof action arising from dealings entirely distinct from those\nactivities\"\n\n[0m[1mRule: Predictability rationale for limiting general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case reasoning in Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV.B,\nwith Hertz Corp. v. Friend cited for the principle that \"simple\njurisdictional rules … promote greater predictability\"\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: General jurisdiction must be limited enough that\nout-of-state defendants can \"structure their primary conduct\nwith some minimum assurance as to where that conduct will and\nwill not render them liable to suit\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Why must general jurisdiction be confined to a\nfew clear forums?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Not applicable, because this is a policy\nrationale that supports the at-home rule\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Courts use it to reject jurisdictional theories\nthat would expose a corporation to all-purpose suit in many\nstates; the Court labeled such theories \"exorbitant exercises of\nall-purpose jurisdiction\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated in the reading\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: This rationale works alongside the paradigm-forum\nrule, whose bases are valued because they are unique and easily\nascertainable\n\n[0m[1mRule: Attribution of subsidiary contacts to parent for general[0m\n[0m[1mjurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding of Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV.A, and\nthe textbook's bullet-point summary (\"A corporation is not\nsubject to general jurisdiction in forum state just because its\nagent or subsidiary is\")\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A foreign parent corporation is not subject to\ngeneral jurisdiction in a forum just because its subsidiary or\nagent is; the Ninth Circuit's agency test, which asked whether\nthe subsidiary's services were \"important\" to the parent, cannot\nbe sustained\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can a subsidiary's in-state contacts be\nimputed to its foreign parent so that the parent is subject to\ngeneral jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The Court left the governing standard open, but\nidentified two competing tests:\n    1. Alter ego test, urged by Daimler and adopted by \"several\n    Courts of Appeals\": contacts are imputed only when the\n    subsidiary is \"so dominated by\" the parent \"as to be its\n    alter ego\"\n    2. Agency test, used by the Ninth Circuit: a \"less rigorous\"\n    test that the Court did not approve\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Invoked when plaintiffs try to reach a foreign\nparent through an in-state subsidiary; the Ninth Circuit's\n\"importance\" inquiry \"stacks the deck\" because it \"will always\nyield a pro-jurisdiction answer,\" since anything done through a\nsubsidiary is something the parent would presumably do \"by other\nmeans,\" and so it would subject foreign corporations to general\njurisdiction \"whenever they have an in-state subsidiary or\naffiliate\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m:\n    - The Court expressly did \"not pass judgment on invocation\n    of an agency theory in the context of general jurisdiction\";\n    it decided only that the Ninth Circuit's analysis could not\n    be sustained \"in no event\"\n    - Agency can be limited to particular purposes: \"One may be\n    an agent for some business purposes and not others\" (2A\n    C.J.S., Agency § 43), so a subsidiary \"might be its parent's\n    agent for claims arising in the place where the subsidiary\n    operates, yet not its agent regarding claims arising\n    elsewhere\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: An alter ego relationship involves domination,\nwhile agency is narrower and can be purpose-specific; in\nDaimler, plaintiffs never argued alter ego\n\n[0m[1mRule: Imputed at-home status still requires the defendant itself[0m\n[0m[1mto be at home[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding of Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV.B\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Even if a subsidiary is at home in the forum and\nits contacts are imputed to the parent, general jurisdiction\nover the parent exists only if the combined contacts render the\nparent itself at home there\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: If a subsidiary's contacts are attributed to\nthe parent, is the parent then automatically subject to general\njurisdiction where the subsidiary is at home?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The parent's affiliations with the forum,\nincluding any imputed contacts, must render the parent\n\"essentially at home\" in the forum\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This was the Court's alternative and dispositive\nground; Daimler's contacts, even including MBUSA's, were \"slim\"\nrelative to its worldwide business (MBUSA's California sales\nwere 2.4% of Daimler's worldwide sales), so Daimler was not at\nhome in California\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: None stated in the reading\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Unlike the attribution rule above, this rule\nassumes imputation and asks whether the result is enough\n\n[0m[1mRule: International comity in the fair play and substantial[0m\n[0m[1mjustice analysis[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case reasoning in Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV.C\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The risks to international comity from an expansive\nview of general jurisdiction \"reinforce\" the conclusion that\nsubjecting a foreign corporation to general jurisdiction would\nnot accord with the \"fair play and substantial justice\" due\nprocess requires\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Do relations with foreign nations bear on\nwhether general jurisdiction over a foreign corporation is\nconsistent with due process?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Not applicable, because this is a supporting\nconsideration rather than an independent test\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The Court noted that other nations do not share\nan \"uninhibited approach\"; in the European Union, a corporation\nmay generally be sued where it is \"domiciled,\" meaning its\n\"statutory seat,\" \"central administration,\" or \"principal place\nof business\"; the Solicitor General reported that foreign\nobjections to expansive general jurisdiction \"have in the past\nimpeded negotiations of international agreements on the\nreciprocal recognition and enforcement of judgments\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: In Daimler, comity \"reinforces\" the at-home\nanalysis; the Court did not treat it as an independent basis for\ndecision\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The EU's \"domicile\" concept parallels the Court's\nparadigm bases of place of incorporation and principal place of\nbusiness\n\n[0m[1mRule: Uncontested points taken as given[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case narrative in Daimler AG v. Bauman, Part IV\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Points the parties agreed on, or failed to contest,\nin the proceedings below are taken as given for purposes of the\ndecision\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How does the Supreme Court treat\njurisdictional issues the parties did not raise or contest\nbelow?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The point was conceded or not contested by a\nparty in the lower courts\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: In Daimler, plaintiffs' failure to argue specific\njurisdiction or alter ego, and their failure to challenge the\nfinding that Daimler's own contacts were \"too sporadic,\" were\ntaken as given; Daimler's failure to object that MBUSA was at\nhome in California led the Court to assume, \"for purposes of\nthis decision only,\" that MBUSA was at home there\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The assumption is limited to the case; it is not a\nholding that MBUSA is actually at home in California\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: An assumption made for the purposes of decision\ndiffers from a holding; exam answers should not cite Daimler as\nholding that MBUSA is at home in California\n\n[0m[1mAt a glance: general vs. specific jurisdiction in Daimler[0m\n+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n|                    | [0m[1mGeneral[0m            | [0m[1mSpecific[0m           |\n|                    | [0m[1m(all-purpose)[0m      | [0m[1m(conduct-linked)[0m   |\n+------------------",
        "--+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Scope              | \"Any and all       | Claims connected   |\n|                    | claims\" against    | to forum activity  |\n|                    | the defendant      |                    |\n+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Test               | Affiliations so    | Episode-in-suit in |\n|                    | continuous and     | forum, or          |\n|                    | systematic as to   | purposeful         |\n|                    | render the         | availment with     |\n|                    | defendant          | activities giving  |\n|                    | \"essentially at    | rise to the        |\n|                    | home\"              | liabilities sued   |\n|                    |                    | on                 |\n+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Paradigm forums    | Individual:        | No paradigm forum  |\n|                    | domicile;          | stated in the      |\n|                    | corporation: place | reading            |\n|                    | of incorporation,  |                    |\n|                    | principal place of |                    |\n|                    | business           |                    |\n+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Post-International | Not stretched      | \"Centerpiece of    |\n| Shoe trend         | \"beyond limits     | modern             |\n|                    | traditionally      | jurisdiction       |\n|                    | recognized\"        | theory\"            |\n+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n| Role in Daimler    | The only theory    | Never invoked by   |\n|                    | plaintiffs         | plaintiffs         |\n|                    | pursued, and       |                    |\n|                    | rejected           |                    |\n+--------------------+--------------------+--------------------+\n\n[0m[1m2. Case Narrative[0m\n[0m[1mDaimler AG v. Bauman[0m\nDaimler, the petitioner, is DaimlerChrysler Aktiengesellschaft,\na German public stock company headquartered in Stuttgart that\nmakes Mercedes-Benz vehicles in Germany. It is the defendant.\nBauman stands for the respondents: twenty-two residents of\nArgentina who are the plaintiffs. The opinion does not describe\nthe individual named Bauman separately. The plaintiffs are\nworkers of Mercedes-Benz Argentina (MB Argentina), or people\nclosely related to those workers.\nTwo other companies matter even though neither was sued. MB\nArgentina was Daimler's Argentinian subsidiary; during the\nrelevant period it was wholly owned by Daimler's predecessor in\ninterest. Mercedes-Benz USA, LLC (MBUSA) is an indirect\nsubsidiary of Daimler, organized in Delaware with its principal\nplace of business in New Jersey. MBUSA is Daimler's exclusive\nimporter and distributor in the United States. It buys\nMercedes-Benz cars from Daimler in Germany, imports them, and\nsells them to independent dealerships across the country,\nincluding in California. MBUSA has several California\nfacilities: a regional office in Costa Mesa, a Vehicle\nPreparation Center in Carson, and a Classic Center in Irvine. It\nis the largest supplier of luxury vehicles to the California\nmarket. Over 10% of all new-vehicle sales in the U.S. happen in\nCalifornia, and MBUSA's California sales account for 2.4% of\nDaimler's worldwide sales. A General Distributor Agreement\ndescribes MBUSA as an \"independent contractor\" that buys and\nsells vehicles \"as an independent business for its own account.\"\nThe agreement says MBUSA is not Daimler's agent, partner, joint\nventurer, or employee and cannot bind Daimler.\nThe underlying events took place in Argentina during the\nmilitary dictatorship of 1976–1983, known as the \"Dirty War.\"\nThe opinion gives no specific dates for individual incidents.\nAccording to the complaint, MB Argentina worked with Argentinian\nstate security forces to kidnap, detain, torture, and kill\ncertain MB Argentina workers, including plaintiffs or people\nclosely related to them. The events centered on MB Argentina's\nplant in Gonzalez Catan, Argentina. None of the alleged\ncollaboration happened in California or anywhere else in the\nUnited States. The opinion does not say what motivated MB\nArgentina or why particular workers were targeted. As the\ntextbook's introductory note puts it, the alleged wrongdoer (MB\nArgentina) had no connection to California and was not sued. The\ncompany with a clear California connection (MBUSA) had nothing\nto do with the alleged wrongdoing and was not sued either. The\nactual defendant (Daimler) had no direct connection to\nCalifornia or to the alleged wrongdoing.\nIn 2004, the twenty-two Argentinian plaintiffs sued Daimler in\nthe U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California.\nThey sought damages for the alleged human-rights violations\nunder the laws of the United States, California, and Argentina.\nThey tried to hold Daimler vicariously liable for MB Argentina's\nconduct and based jurisdiction mainly on MBUSA's California\ncontacts.\n[0m[1m3. Cases[0m\n[0m[1mCase Name: Bauman v. Daimler AG (captioned in the Supreme Court[0m\n[0m[1mas Daimler AG v. Bauman)[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1114-22\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure, Class 33, Specific & General\nJurisdiction\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - In 2004, twenty-two Argentinian residents sued Daimler, a\n    German public stock company headquartered in Stuttgart, in\n    the Northern District of California\n    - The complaint alleged that, during Argentina's 1976–1983\n    \"Dirty War,\" MB Argentina collaborated with state security\n    forces to kidnap, detain, torture, and kill MB Argentina\n    workers, including plaintiffs or their close relatives, at\n    or around its Gonzalez Catan plant\n    - No part of the alleged conduct took place in California or\n    anywhere in the United States; there was no California\n    connection to the \"atrocities, perpetrators, or victims\"\n    - Daimler was the only corporate defendant, sued on a\n    vicarious-liability theory for the acts of MB Argentina, a\n    subsidiary wholly owned at the relevant time by Daimler's\n    predecessor in interest\n    - Plaintiffs sought damages under U.S., California, and\n    Argentinian law\n    - Jurisdiction was based on the California contacts of\n    MBUSA, an indirect Daimler subsidiary organized in Delaware\n    with its principal place of business in New Jersey, and\n    Daimler's exclusive U.S. importer and distributor\n    - MBUSA has California facilities in Costa Mesa (regional\n    office), Carson (Vehicle Preparation Center), and Irvine\n    (Classic Center); it is the largest supplier of luxury\n    vehicles in California; MBUSA's California sales are 2.4% of\n    Daimler's worldwide sales\n    - The General Distributor Agreement labels MBUSA an\n    \"independent contractor\" acting \"for its own account,\" and\n    not Daimler's agent, with no authority to bind Daimler\n    - Neither Daimler nor MBUSA is incorporated in California or\n    has its principal place of business there\n    - Plaintiffs' counsel conceded that, under their theory,\n    Polish plaintiffs injured when a Daimler-made car overturned\n    in Poland could bring a design-defect suit in California\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - U.S. District Court for the Northern District of\n    California: after jurisdictional discovery on plaintiffs'\n    agency allegations, granted Defendant Daimler's motion to\n    dismiss for want of personal jurisdiction, holding that\n    Daimler's own California affiliations were insufficient for\n    all-purpose jurisdiction and declining to attribute MBUSA's\n    California contacts to Daimler because plaintiffs failed to\n    show that MBUSA acted as Daimler's agent\n    - Appeal of right: Plaintiffs-Appellants sought review in\n    the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit on the\n    grounds that MBUSA acted as Daimler's agent, so MBUSA's\n    California contacts should be imputed to Daimler to support\n    general jurisdiction, without challenging the holding that\n    Daimler's own contacts were too sporadic\n    - U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit: reversed,\n    holding that MBUSA acted as Daimler's agent for\n    jurisdictional purposes because MBUSA's services were\n    \"important\" to Daimler, and attributing MBUSA's California\n    contacts to Daimler; rehearing en banc was later denied over\n    Judge O'Scannlain's dissent\n    - Petition for writ of certiorari: Daimler,\n    Defendant-Appellee below, as Petitioner sought review in the\n    U.S. Supreme Court on the grounds that the Due Process\n    Clause bars California from exercising general jurisdiction\n    over it and that a subsidiary's contacts may be imputed to a\n    parent only when the subsidiary is the parent's alter ego\n    - U.S. Supreme Court: Daimler (defendant below) is the\n    petitioner; the twenty-two Argentinian residents (plaintiffs\n    below) are the respondents; certiorari granted to address\n    whether the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment\n    precludes the District Court from exercising jurisdiction\n    over Daimler given the absence of any California connection\n    to the atrocities, perpetrators, or victims described in the\n    complaint\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth\nAmendment allows a California federal court to exercise general\njurisdiction over a German corporation, based on the California\ncontacts of its U.S. subsidiary, for claims by foreign\nplaintiffs arising entirely from a different subsidiary's\nconduct in Argentina?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The U.S. Supreme Court held that due process bars\ngeneral jurisdiction over Daimler in California; even assuming\nMBUSA is at home in California and its contacts are imputable to\nDaimler, Daimler is not \"at home\" in California and cannot be\nsued there for injuries attributed to MB Argentina's conduct in\nArgentina\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(A): Federal courts ordinarily\n    follow state law in determining the bounds of their\n    jurisdiction over persons\n    - Cal. Civ. Proc. Code § 410.10: California courts may\n    exercise personal jurisdiction to the full extent the U.S.\n    Constitution permits\n    - General and specific jurisdiction distinction: General\n    jurisdiction lets a court hear any and all claims against a\n    defendant, while specific jurisdiction depends on a link\n    between the forum and the claim\n    - Divergent trajectories of general and specific\n    jurisdiction after International Shoe: Specific jurisdiction\n    has expanded since International Shoe, but general\n    jurisdiction has not been stretched beyond traditionally\n    recognized limits\n    - Specific jurisdiction under International Shoe:\n    Jurisdiction is proper where in-state activities are\n    continuous and systematic and also give rise to the\n    liabilities sued on\n    - At home standard for general jurisdiction: A corporation\n    is subject to general jurisdiction only where its\n    affiliations are so continuous and systematic as to render\n    it essentially at home in the forum state\n    - Paradigm forums for general jurisdiction: An individual's\n    domicile, and a corporation's place of incorporation and\n    principal place of business, are the paradigm bases for\n    general jurisdiction\n    - Substantial, continuous, and systematic course of business\n    insufficient for general jurisdiction: A corporation is not\n    subject to general jurisdiction in every state where it\n    engages in a substantial, continuous, and systematic course\n    of business\n    - Predictability rationale for limiting general\n    jurisdiction: General jurisdiction must be limited so\n    defendants can structure their primary conduct with some\n    minimum assurance about where they can be sued\n    - Attribution of subsidiary contacts to parent for general\n    jurisdiction: A parent is not subject to general\n    jurisdiction merely because its subsidiary is, and an agency\n    test based on the \"importance\" of the subsidiary's services\n    cannot be sustained\n    - Imputed at-home status still requires the defendant itself\n    to be at home: Even with a subsidiary's contacts imputed,\n    the parent must itself be essentially at home in the forum\n    - International comity in the fair play and substantial\n    justice analysis: Risks to international comity reinforce\n    the conclusion that expansive general jurisdiction over a\n    foreign corporation offends fair play and substantial\n    justice\n    - Uncontested points taken as given: Points the parties\n    agreed on or failed to contest below are taken as given for\n    the decision\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m:\n    - The Supreme Court reasoned that, because California's\n    long-arm statute reaches as far as the Constitution allows,\n    the only question was whether federal due process permitted\n    jurisdiction\n    - The Supreme Court framed the case under the Goodyear\n    distinction; plaintiffs never invoked specific jurisdiction,\n    did not challenge the holding that Daimler's own contacts\n    were too sporadic, and never argued alter ego, while Daimler\n    did not contest that MBUSA was at home in California, so the\n    Court assumed that point for this decision only\n    - The Supreme Court noted that, since International Shoe,\n    specific jurisdiction has expanded while general\n    jurisdiction has not been stretched beyond traditional\n    limits\n    - On attribution (Part IV.A), the Supreme Court declined to\n    decide whether an agency theory can ever support general\n    jurisdiction, but rejected the Ninth Circuit's version:\n    asking whether a subsidiary's services are \"important,\"\n    measured by whether the parent would perform them itself,\n    \"stacks the deck\" and would subject foreign corporations to\n    general jurisdiction \"whenever they have an in-state\n    subsidiary or affiliate,\" which goes beyond even the\n    \"sprawling view of general jurisdiction\" rejected in\n    Goodyear\n    - The Supreme Court added that the Ninth Circuit ignored\n    that agency can be purpose-specific: a subsidiary might be\n    the parent's agent for claims arising where it operates but\n    not for claims arising elsewhere\n    - On the at-home question (Part IV.B), the Supreme Court\n    held that, even assuming MBUSA is at home in California and\n    its contacts are imputable to Daimler, Daimler's \"slim\n    contacts\" do not make it at home there\n    - The Supreme Court explained that Goodyear's paradigm\n    bases, place of incorporation and principal place of\n    business, are valuable because they are unique and easily\n    ascertainable and give plaintiffs at least one clear forum;\n    neither Daimler nor MBUSA is incorporated in California or\n    has its principal place of business there\n    - The Supreme Court rejected plaintiffs' proposed test of\n    \"substantial, continuous, and systematic course of business\"\n    as \"unacceptably grasping,\" reasoning that International\n    Shoe used \"continuous and systematic\" to describe specific\n    jurisdiction, and that general jurisdiction requires\n    affiliations so continuous and systematic as to render the\n    defendant \"essentially at home\"\n    - The Supreme Court observed that, if Daimler's California\n    activities sufficed, the same \"global reach\" would exist in\n    every state where MBUSA's sales are sizable; such\n    \"exorbitant\" jurisdiction would deny defendants the\n    predictability to \"structure their primary conduct\"\n    - On comity (Part IV.C), the Supreme Court noted that the\n    Ninth Circuit \"paid little heed\" to international comity;\n    the EU limits general jurisdiction to a corporation's\n    domicile, and foreign objections to expansive U.S. general\n    jurisdiction have impeded treaty negotiations on recognizing\n    and enforcing judgments, which reinforces that jurisdiction\n    here would offend \"fair play and substantial justice\"\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m:\n    - Daimler itself was present in California, supported by\n    declarations and exhibits submitted in opposition to the\n    motion to dismiss; this argument was abandoned on appeal\n    - Alternatively, MBUSA should be treated as Daimler's agent\n    for jurisdictional purposes, so MBUSA's California contacts\n    should be attributed to Daimler\n    - California is a place where Daimler may be sued on \"any\n    and all claims against it, wherever in the world the claims\n    may arise\"\n    - General jurisdiction should exist in every state where a\n    corporation \"engages in a substantial, continuous, and\n    systematic course of business,\" looking beyond Goodyear's\n    exemplar bases\n    - Plaintiffs relied on MBUSA's large California presence:\n    multiple facilities, its position as the largest\n    luxury-vehicle supplier in California, and California sales\n    making up 2.4% of Daimler's worldwide sales\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m:\n    - Daimler's own affiliations with California are\n    insufficient for all-purpose jurisdiction\n    - A subsidiary's jurisdictional contacts can be imputed to\n    its parent only when the subsidiary is so dominated by the\n    parent as to be its alter ego, a test several Courts of\n    Appeals have adopted; plaintiffs never alleged alter ego\n    - MBUSA is not Daimler's agent; the General Distributor\n    Agreement makes MBUSA an independent contractor acting for\n    its own account with no authority to bind Daimler\n    - Exercising general jurisdiction over Daimler in California\n    for claims arising in Argentina violates the Due Process\n    Clause\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The U.S. Supreme Court reversed the judgment of\nthe U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit; Justice\nSotomayor concurred in the judgment in an opinion the casebook\nomits\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m:\n    - Daimler confirms that Goodyear's \"essentially at home\"\n    test is the governing test for general jurisdiction over\n    corporations and rejects the \"doing substantial, continuous,\n    and systematic business\" approach\n    - It establishes that place of incorporation and principal\n    place of business are the paradigm at-home forums, while\n    leaving open, without identifying, other possible at-home\n    forums\n    - It teaches that a parent corporation is not subject to\n    general jurisdiction just because its subsidiary or agent\n    is, and rejects the Ninth Circuit's \"importance\"-based\n    agency test, while leaving open whether any agency theory\n    could support general jurisdiction\n    - It reinforces the narrative point that specific\n    jurisdiction has expanded under International Shoe while\n    general jurisdiction has not\n    - It shows how a fact pattern with foreign plaintiffs, a\n    foreign defendant, and foreign events makes general\n    jurisdiction the only possible route, and why that route\n    failed\n    - The textbook presents Daimler as \"a nice review of the\n    central concepts in personal jurisdiction\" and as\n    representing \"the outer limits\" of how some lower courts had\n    read general jurisdiction before Goodyear, since the\n    lower-court decisions came first; the casebook asks whether\n    Daimler \"break[s] new ground,\" whether it was an easy or\n    hard case, and why the Supreme Court agreed to hear it, but\n    the reading does not answer those questions, so be ready to\n    discuss them in class\n[0m[1m4. Definitions[0m\n[0m[1mGeneral jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: All-purpose jurisdiction: a court's authority to\nhear \"any and all claims\" against a defendant, wherever in the\nworld the claims arose, available only where the defendant is\n\"essentially at home\"\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was the only theory plaintiffs pursued; the\nCourt held Daimler was not subject to it in California\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It differs from specific jurisdiction because it\ndoes not require any connec",
        "tion between the claim and the forum\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mSpecific jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Conduct-linked jurisdiction over claims connected\nto the defendant's forum activities, such as when the\nepisode-in-suit occurred in the forum or the defendant\npurposefully availed itself of the forum and those activities\ngave rise to the liabilities sued on\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Described as \"the centerpiece of modern\njurisdiction theory\"; plaintiffs never tried to invoke it\nbecause nothing in the case touched California\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is claim-specific, while general jurisdiction\nis all-purpose\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mEssentially at home[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The Goodyear standard for general jurisdiction:\naffiliations with the forum state so constant and pervasive, so\n\"continuous and systematic,\" that the corporation is fairly\nregarded as at home there\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the controlling test that Daimler failed\nin California\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is more demanding than having contacts that\nare merely \"in some sense 'continuous and systematic'\"\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mParadigm forum[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An exemplar place where a defendant is clearly\nsubject to general jurisdiction: the domicile for an individual,\nand the place of incorporation and principal place of business\nfor a corporation\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: These forums are unique and easily\nascertainable, and they guarantee plaintiffs at least one clear\nand certain forum; neither Daimler nor MBUSA had a paradigm\nforum in California\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Paradigm forums are examples, not an exclusive\nlist; Goodyear \"did not hold\" that general jurisdiction exists\nonly there\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mDomicile[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The paradigm forum for general jurisdiction over\nan individual; in the EU's usage described in the opinion, a\ncorporation's \"domicile\" refers to its statutory seat, central\nadministration, or principal place of business\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: A corporation's place of incorporation and\nprincipal place of business serve as the \"equivalent place\" to\nan individual's domicile; the EU's corporate domicile rule\nsupports the comity argument\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: For individuals, domicile is the at-home place;\nfor corporations, the U.S. analogues are place of incorporation\nand principal place of business\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mPrincipal place of business[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: One of the two paradigm bases for corporate\ngeneral jurisdiction; the reading does not define it further,\nbut cites Hertz Corp. v. Friend for the value of simple,\npredictable jurisdictional rules\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: MBUSA's principal place of business is New\nJersey, not California, and Daimler's headquarters is in\nStuttgart\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It differs from a place where a corporation\nmerely has facilities or substantial sales, like MBUSA's\nCalifornia offices\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mPlace of incorporation[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The jurisdiction under whose laws a corporation is\norganized; one of the two paradigm bases for corporate general\njurisdiction\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Daimler is a German company and MBUSA is a\nDelaware entity; neither is incorporated in California\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Like principal place of business, it is unique\nand easily ascertainable, unlike volume-of-business measures\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mLong-arm statute[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A state statute defining how far that state's\ncourts may exercise personal jurisdiction over out-of-state\ndefendants\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: California's statute, Cal. Civ. Proc. Code §\n410.10, reaches to the constitutional limit, so the case turned\nentirely on due process\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The statutory limit is separate from the\nconstitutional due process limit, but California's statute\nmerges the two\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mDue Process Clause[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The Fourteenth Amendment provision that limits a\nstate's assertion of adjudicatory authority over defendants\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The question presented was whether it precluded\njurisdiction over Daimler; the Court held that it did\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is a constitutional ceiling, while long-arm\nstatutes are statutory grants that may reach that ceiling\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mFair play and substantial justice[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The due process standard that personal\njurisdiction must satisfy, as quoted in the reading\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Court held that subjecting Daimler to\ngeneral jurisdiction in California would not accord with it, a\nconclusion reinforced by comity concerns\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: In Daimler, it serves as the overarching due\nprocess standard, with the at-home test and comity as\nconsiderations within it\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mInternational comity[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Considerations of \"international rapport,\" meaning\nrespect for other nations' approaches and the effect of U.S.\njurisdictional rules on foreign relations\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Court faulted the Ninth Circuit for ignoring\ncomity, noting EU limits and foreign objections that have\nimpeded treaty negotiations on recognizing and enforcing\njudgments\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is a reinforcing consideration, not the\nprimary test\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mAlter ego[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A relationship in which a subsidiary is \"so\ndominated by\" its parent as to be the parent's alter ego;\nDaimler and several Courts of Appeals treat it as the only basis\nfor imputing a subsidiary's contacts to a parent\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Plaintiffs never argued that MBUSA was Daimler's\nalter ego, which pushed them toward the weaker agency theory\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It requires domination and is more rigorous than\nthe Ninth Circuit's agency test\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mAgency[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A relationship in which one party acts on\nanother's behalf; agencies \"come in many sizes and shapes,\" and\none may be an agent for some business purposes but not others\n(2A C.J.S., Agency § 43)\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Ninth Circuit imputed MBUSA's contacts to\nDaimler on an agency theory; the Supreme Court rejected that\nanalysis without deciding whether agency can ever support\ngeneral jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is less rigorous than alter ego, and it can be\npurpose-specific, for example covering claims arising where the\nsubsidiary operates but not claims arising elsewhere\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mSubsidiary[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A distinct corporate entity owned by a parent\ncorporation; the reading describes MB Argentina as wholly owned\nby Daimler's predecessor at the relevant time and MBUSA as an\n\"indirect\" subsidiary of Daimler\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Plaintiffs tried to reach Daimler through one\nsubsidiary's (MBUSA's) forum contacts, for the conduct of\nanother subsidiary (MB Argentina)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A subsidiary is legally separate from its parent;\nits contacts are not automatically the parent's\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mIndependent contractor[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: As used in the General Distributor Agreement, an\nentity that buys and sells \"as an independent business for its\nown account,\" not as an agent, partner, joint venturer, or\nemployee, and with no authority to bind the other party\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was the contractual label for MBUSA's\nrelationship with Daimler, which undercut the agency theory; the\nDistrict Court found no agency\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is contrasted with an agent, who can act on\nbehalf of a principal\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mAktiengesellschaft[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A German public stock company, the corporate form\nof Daimler\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It identifies Daimler as a foreign corporation\nheadquartered in Stuttgart\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: None drawn in the reading\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mVicarious liability[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Liability imposed on one party for another's\nwrongdoing\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Plaintiffs sought to hold Daimler vicariously\nliable for MB Argentina's alleged conduct\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It concerns liability on the merits, while\nattribution of contacts concerns jurisdiction; the reading\ntreats imputing contacts as a separate jurisdictional question\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mJurisdictional discovery[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Discovery the court allows on facts relevant to\nwhether it has personal jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The District Court allowed it on plaintiffs'\nagency allegations before dismissing the case\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is limited to jurisdictional facts rather than\nthe merits\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n\n[0m[1mExorbitant jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The Court's term for an over-broad exercise of\npersonal jurisdiction, such as all-purpose jurisdiction wherever\na corporation's sales are sizable\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Court called plaintiffs' theory, illustrated\nby the Poland car-crash hypothetical, \"exorbitant\" and barred by\ndue process\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is contrasted with the limited, predictable\nparadigm forums\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Daimler AG v. Bauman\n"
    )
    val codePoints: IntArray
        get() = textChunks.joinToString("").codePoints().toArray()
}

interface GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String
}

class SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy : GlyphAssemblyStrategy {
    override fun assemble(codePoints: IntArray): String {
        val builder = StringBuilder()
        for (codePoint in codePoints) {
            builder.appendCodePoint(codePoint)
        }
        return builder.toString()
    }
}

class DocumentAssemblyEngine(private val strategy: GlyphAssemblyStrategy) {
    fun render(): String = strategy.assemble(CodePointRepository.codePoints)
}

class ConsoleOutputSink {
    fun writeLine(payload: String) = println(payload)
}

class DocumentCompilerFacade {
    private val engine = DocumentAssemblyEngine(SequentialGlyphAssemblyStrategy())
    private val sink = ConsoleOutputSink()

    fun execute() {
        sink.writeLine(engine.render())
    }
}

fun main() {
    DocumentCompilerFacade().execute()
}
